    "# Results {#sec-results}\n",
    "\n",
    "## Structure, contents, and where the data are held {#sec-structure}\n",
    "\n",
    "This data resource is a zip archive containing three comma-separated files, deposited on Zenodo under a DOI that will be minted at acceptance<!-- OUTLINE run-list item 10 -->. The high-level characteristics of the files are described in @tbl-release: a simulated registry file of `{python} registry_simulants` simulated people and two finder files of `{python} finder_500k_simulants` and `{python} finder_100k_simulants` simulated people respectively. All three are drawn from Florida residents in the simulated 2020, 2030 or 2040 census, a pool of `{python} fl_pool` distinct simulants. \n",
    "\n",
    "Every row of each file carries a `simulant_id`, the identifier of the simulated person it describes, and the identifier provides a \"ground truth\" linkage of the same person in all three files; this is the true and correct linkage against which any other linkage of the files can be scored. The files also contain the fields that record linkage typically relies on: first name, middle initial, last name, date of birth, sex, race/ethnicity, street number, street name, unit number, city, state, ZIP code and social security number (SSN). Registry rows also carry a diagnosis date, which is one of these three values: 2020, 2030 or 2040. Addresses are for Florida cities and ZIP codes, designed to have street addresses that are *not* real street locations. The registry has `{python} registry_rows` rows for its `{python} registry_simulants` people, and the finder files have `{python} n(files[\"finder_500k.csv\"][\"rows\"])` and `{python} n(files[\"finder_100k.csv\"][\"rows\"])` rows for `{python} finder_500k_simulants` and `{python} finder_100k_simulants` people; the few people with two rows are duplicated records (@sec-methods).\n",
    "\n",
    "The larger finder file shares `{python} overlap_500k_simulants` people with the registry, `{python} pct(match_prevalence_500k, 0)` of its records, and the smaller shares `{python} overlap_100k_simulants`, the same `{python} pct(match_prevalence_100k, 0)` share; the two finder files share no people with each other. Alongside the files, the deposit carries a manifest of MD5 and SHA-256 digests (@tbl-release gives the MD5) and the configuration file that names every seed and design target, and the pipeline that built them is archived under its own DOI<!-- OUTLINE run-list item 10: code DOI -->. A second build from that configuration reproduced all three files byte for byte."
   ]
